/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Radar means radio detection and ranging.
   - R is slant range in metres, c = 3 × 10⁸ m/s is the speed of light, and t is the echo delay in seconds measured from the transmitted pulse.
   - Echo power drops as the fourth power of range: double the distance and only one-sixteenth comes back.
   - Rmax is the maximum detection range in metres; Pt is the transmitted peak power in watts; G is the antenna gain, a pure number; λ is the wavelength in metres; σ is the radar cross-section of the target in square metres, set by shape and mat
   - Pulse width and pulse repetition frequency set two different limits, and they are the pair students mix up.
   - ΔR is the range resolution in metres and τ the pulse width in seconds; Ru is the maximum unambiguous range in metres and PRF the pulse repetition frequency in hertz, that is, how many pulses leave the antenna each second.
   - A short pulse separates targets in range; a low PRF gives a long unambiguous range.
   - fd is the Doppler shift in hertz, vr is the radial (closing) component of target speed in m/s, and λ is the wavelength in metres.
   - Primary surveillance radar (PSR) works on the skin echo.
   - Secondary surveillance radar (SSR) is a conversation.
*/
