/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - In CFIT an airworthy aircraft, under control, is flown into ground, water or an obstacle the crew did not see in time, usually at night or in cloud after a small altitude or track error.
   - Two heights exist in the cockpit.
   - Every reactive warning below is built from RA plus sink rate, airspeed, gear and flap position, and — in one mode only — the ILS glideslope.
   - Classic GPWS is a set of seven modes.
   - Mode 1 works on barometric sink rate, gated by RA: the allowed sink rate falls as RA falls.
   - Mode 5 is the only reactive mode that needs a ground aid: it is armed when an ILS is tuned and the aircraft sits more than about 1.3 dots below the beam at low RA.
   - A terrain awareness and warning system (TAWS), also sold as enhanced GPWS, keeps the reactive modes and adds forward-looking terrain avoidance (FLTA).
   - Caution is amber, on the display and as a soft voice, about 60 s before predicted impact: "TERRAIN AHEAD".
   - Inhibits exist because a warning that cries wolf gets switched off.
*/
